course: dictionary
lesson: 10136
title: Thresh: beating grain and thrashing about
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «The farmers threshed the wheat on the barn floor.» The clues are wheat, barn and farmers, so threshed must be farm work. It means they beat the grain to knock the seeds loose from the stalks.
explain: Now read «The Crocodile threshed his tail like an oar.» Nobody is harvesting here. A tail is moving wildly, so thresh can also mean to thrash about. This meaning is old and less common, so today people usually say thrash.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "Beat the wheat to free the seeds" fits the farm sentence. "Thrash his tail like an oar" fits the crocodile. The context tells you which meaning the writer chose.
fact: «Thresh» is a verb: thresh, threshes, threshed, threshing.
fact: «Thresh» means to beat or shake cut wheat, oats or other grain to separate the seeds from the stalks and husks.
fact: Long ago, farmers threshed grain by beating it with a flail on the barn floor.
fact: Later, steam threshing machines came round to farms, and today combine harvesters cut and thresh at once.
fact: «Thresh» can also mean to move about wildly, but this use is old or less common; people usually say «thrash».
exercise: «The farmers threshed the oats on the barn floor.» What does threshed mean here? | *Beat the grain to free the seeds | Sold the oats at the market | Painted the barn walls | Built a fence around the field
exercise: «The Crocodile threshed his tail like an oar.» What does threshed mean here? | Dried in the sun | *Thrashed about wildly | Gently stroked | Tied up tightly
exercise: Today, which word would most people use for the wild-moving meaning of thresh? | Threshold | Thrill | Thrive | *Thrash
test: Which tool did farmers long ago use to thresh grain by beating it? | Tractor | Scythe | *Flail | Plough
test: Which machine came to farms later to thresh grain? | Rowing boat | Steam train | Hot-air balloon | *Steam threshing machine
exam: Which sentence means the grain was beaten to free its seeds? | *The farmers threshed the wheat on the barn floor | The boy threshed his legs in the pool | The dog threshed in the mud, happy | The Crocodile threshed his tail like an oar
